# Provenance — decoder_panic_surface_2026-08-06

## What this is

The audit + conversion of panics reachable from a crafted bitstream on the
DECODE path (`aom-decode` and the `aom-dsp` modules it reaches), the widening
of the stable fuzz sweep that was used to hunt for more, and the reach
measurement that says how much the sweep's green actually covers.

## Box

    host        mac (Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores), darwin-arm64
    kernel      Darwin 25.5.0
    macOS       26.5.2
    rustc       1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14), stable-aarch64-apple-darwin
    commit      020d3ef (sweep widening), 1787310 (panic conversions);
                branch harden/decoder-panic-surface off f1d5bc6-era main
    load        ANOTHER HEAVY JOB WAS RUNNING on this box for the whole session

## nice / QoS

Everything here ran under `nice -n 19` and every cargo invocation used `-j 4`
with `RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache`, per the shared-box rule.

**No number in the `.md` is a timing.** On Darwin, `nice -n 19` maps to
background QoS (E-cores) and has been measured at ~40x wall-clock penalty
versus `nice -n 5` in this repo, so a `nice -n 19` run is worthless for
performance claims. Every figure reported is an exact COUNT — iterations,
inputs classified, table entries, tests passed — which the scheduler cannot
change. Wall-clock figures that do appear (e.g. "~200 s for
`real_bitstream.rs`") are informational only and must not be quoted as
performance data.

## Static inventory

    python3 ~/tmp/panics/scan2.py

Counts `unwrap( expect( panic! unreachable! todo! unimplemented! assert!
assert_eq! assert_ne!` outside `#[cfg(test)]` blocks and outside `//` comment
lines, over `crates/{aom-decode,aom-dsp,aom-encode,zenav1-aom}/src/**.rs`;
`debug_assert*` is excluded by a prefix check. The `#[cfg(test)]` stripper is a
brace matcher, so a `#[cfg(test)]` block with unbalanced braces inside a string
literal would be miscounted — none exist in this tree. Treat the table in §1 of
the `.md` as an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE, not an exact census: it is a lexical scan,
not a semantic one.

## Fuzz sweep

    FUZZ_SMOKE_ITERS=<n> FUZZ_SMOKE_SEED=<s> FUZZ_CRASH_DIR=~/tmp/panics/... \
      nice -n 19 env RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache cargo test --profile test-fast \
      -p zenav1-aom-decode --test fuzz_sweep -j 4 -- --nocapture

`test-fast` is opt-level 3 WITH `debug-assertions` and `overflow-checks` on, so
the sweep still catches debug-only arithmetic-overflow panics — that is why it
is the right profile for this and why a release-profile sweep would be weaker.

The pre-existing-mutator baseline (5 x 250 000, seeds 1/2/3/7/11) was run
against commit f1d5bc6-era code BEFORE the mutator change; the widened runs are
against 020d3ef and later. Iteration counts and per-seed reach histograms are
in `~/tmp/panics/discover*.log` and `~/tmp/panics/sweep*_s*.log` (scratch, not
committed — the committed evidence is the test itself, which reproduces the
histogram on any box).

## Verification suite

    nice -n 19 env RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache cargo test --profile test-fast \
      -p zenav1-aom-decode -p zenav1-aom-dsp -j 4 --no-fail-fast

459 passed / 0 failed. `tests/real_bitstream.rs` links the in-process libaom
v3.14.1 oracle (`aom-sys-ref`) and asserts byte identity, so "behaviour-
preserving on valid input" is an oracle result, not an inspection.

## What is NOT measured here

* No timing, throughput, or instruction-count claim of any kind (see the QoS
  note above).
* No `AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1` leg. The changes are on the scalar control path
  (guards and panic messages) and touch no SIMD kernel, so the dispatch tier is
  irrelevant to them; the scalar-pin gate was not re-run for this work.
* No encoder-side sweep. `aom-encode`'s panic sites were reviewed for
  bitstream reachability and left alone.
* No coverage instrumentation. "Deep reach" is measured by classifying the
  decoder's own error text, not by line coverage, so it is a lower bound on
  what the sweep executes, not a coverage figure.
